      "kind": "item",
      "label": "Follow up: P26: a library's own new thing is made by name only for the types its project file exports",
      "excerpt": "Follow up: P26: a library's own new thing is made by name only for the types its project file exports",
      "text": "clarify",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-07T22:12:14.764Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-07T22:13:39.767Z",
          "html": "<p>What T41 means, in plain words:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>\n    A library (say, a cards library) can make things of its own kinds: its deck makes cards with <code>a new card</code>.\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    When Solitaire uses that library, you'd want the deck to make <em>Solitaire's</em> card (with Solitaire's own looks and\n    rules), not the library's plain one.\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    Since P26 that happens, but only when the library's project file lists the type in its <code>exports</code>:\n    <code>\"exports\": [\"Card\", \"Deck\"]</code>. Without that line, the deck keeps making the library's own plain cards.\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    I chose that so no existing project's compiled code changed. Making it the rule for every type would have changed 5\n    files in recipe-box, recetario, live-room and sala-en-vivo, for no gain.\n  </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The follow-up question T41 holds: is \"only what <code>exports</code> lists\" the rule you want, or should a library always let the project using it supply its own version of each type?</p>\n<epic-net-effect><ul><li>Nothing changes unless you pick the other rule;  then every library type is made by name, and those 5 files' compiled code changes.</li></ul></epic-net-effect>"
        }
      ]
    },
    "c2": {
      "anchor": "p-choose-from-a-list#field-2",
      "kind": "field",
      "label": "Choose From a List",
      "excerpt": "choose a card from the cards with prompt \"Which card?\" asks the person to pick o",
      "text": "a string type \"card suits\" should work, as should colors (\"pick a color\" could use the spell default colors as color chips).  List of strings should obviously work.",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-19T02:56:07.671Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "for list of views",
      "offset": 300,
      "edited": "2026-11-19T02:56:52.998Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-19T03:06:34.992Z",
          "html": "<p>P66's goal and plan now cover all three:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>A kind's own values, with no <code>from</code>: <code>choose a card suit</code>, Spanish <code>elegir un palo de carta</code>, worded like <code>a random card suit</code>, which Spell already has.</li>\n  <li>Colors: <code>choose a color</code>, Spanish <code>elegir un color</code>, shows Spell UI's 13 colors (the ones <code>notify ... in red</code> takes), each row a <code>&lt;ui-color-chip&gt;</code> beside its name. <code>it</code> is the name, like <code>red</code>.</li>\n  <li>A list of text works like any list: <code>choose one of the levels</code>. Written in place, it's Spell's bracketed list, <code>choose one of [\"easy\", \"medium\", \"hard\"]</code>. A bare <code>\"easy\", \"medium\" and \"hard\"</code> isn't a list in Spell today, so I didn't propose it.</li>\n  <li>Clashes, in step 1: <code>card suit</code> is one name, so the longest name wins over <code>choose a card from the cards</code>. A program's own <code>color</code> kind wins over Spell UI's colors: Solitaire's <code>choose a card color</code> offers red and black only.</li>\n  <li>Both new phrases wait on your OK in step 1, as every new phrase does.</li>\n</ul>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
